pr_comments:
I've been able to narrow down the reproduction to this. Instantiating a `Logger` from 3rd party package `tslog` (unrelated to Fastify, don't know why this is necessary) is required. The reproduction is so weirdly specific I don't even really know why some of the lines are required.

Applying the changes in this PR stop the `Error: "code" is required!` error from happening, so instead of cURL crashing with

```sh
$ localhost:3000/
curl: (52) Empty reply from server
```

The server successfully responds with

```json
{"statusCode":500,"message":"message","error":"Internal Server Error","code":"CODE"}
```

Reproduction:

```js
const createServer = require("fastify");
const createError = require("fastify-error");
const { Logger } = require("tslog");

new Logger();

const fastify = createServer({ logger: true });

const CustomError = createError("code", "message", 500);

fastify.route({
  method: "GET",
  url: "/",
  schema: {
    response: {
      500: {
        type: "object",
        properties: {
          statusCode: {
            type: "number",
          },
          message: {
            type: "string",
          },
          error: {
            type: "string",
          },
          code: {
            type: "string",
          },
        },
        required: ["code", "error", "message", "statusCode"],
        additionalProperties: false,
      },
    },
  },
  handler: (request, reply) => {
    reply.send(new CustomError());
  },
});

fastify.addHook("onError", async () => {});

fastify.listen({ port: 3000 });
```

review_comments:
Your test passes to the `handleError` function an `error` that looks like:

```js
{
  message: 'kaboom',
  statusCode: 400,
  code: 'FST_ERR_HELLO_WORLD'
}
```

Thus, when this condition is hit, the `Object.create(error, { /* additional properties */ })` will create a new object to pass into the serializer who's prototype includes a `code` property. The schema in the test looks for the `code` property, finds it on the prototype, and fills it in correctly.

The following test passes on the `master` branch (i.e. without this PR applied):

```js
"use strict";

const fastify = require("fastify")();

fastify.get(
  "/",
  {
    schema: {
      response: {
        500: {
          type: "object",
          properties: {
            code: { type: "string" },
            error: { type: "string" },
            message: { type: "string" },
            statusCode: { type: "integer" },
          },
          required: ["code", "error", "message", "statusCode"],
          additionalProperties: false,
        },
      },
    },
  },
  (req, res) => {
    const error = new Error("boom");
    error.statusCode = 500;
    error.code = "FST_ERR_HELLO_WORLD";
    res.serializer((data) => JSON.stringify(data)).send(error);
  }
);

fastify.inject({ url: "/", method: "GET" }, (err, res) => {
  if (err) console.error(err);
  console.log(res.json());
});
```

What conditions necessitate this PR? Without a replication in the test, this PR does not seem needed.

It looks like the error is something specifically to do with `fastify-error`. When I do

```js
import createError from 'fastify-error';
const CustomError = createError('FST_ERR_HELLO_WORLD', 'error message', 500);

// ...

reply.send(new CustomError());
```
I can consistently reproduce. Applying the change to include `error.code` in that `reply.js` fixes the bug. Is this still in scope for `fastify/fastify` or should an issue be raised on `fastify/fastify-error`?
